\details{
\code{ModelInfo_MClust$fit_table} uses the fit indices returned by tidyLPA's
mclust backend. AIC, AWE, BIC, CAIC, CLC, KIC, SABIC, and ICL are
likelihood/information criteria, for which lower values are preferred when
comparing candidate fits estimated on the same data. Entropy summarizes
classification separation (higher is better). \code{MinProfileNodeN} and
\code{MaxProfileNodeN} are integer SOM-node counts in the smallest and largest
profile; the corresponding \verb{*Proportion} fields divide those counts by the
total number of SOM nodes. \code{BLRTStatistic} and \code{BLRTPValue} compare a
k-profile model with k - 1 profiles under the same covariance model. A
small p-value supports the added profile's statistical fit, but does not
establish clinical meaning or reproducibility; it is unavailable for k = 1.
The raw \code{BLRTPValue} remains in the table. Its candidate-review panel
displays \code{-log10(BLRTPValue)} with a dashed \code{0.05} p-value
reference at \code{-log10(0.05)}.

Lifecycle settings are intentionally strict: \code{k_range} and
\code{models} are exploratory-only, while \code{final_k} and
\code{final_model} are finalize-only. Supplying settings from both modes is
an error, even when an exploratory setting equals its default.

Stability is assessed by refitting the full SOM/LPA pipeline on independent
participant subsamples drawn without replacement, then mapping the
complete reference cohort onto each refit. \code{StabilityARI_Mean} is the
primary reproducibility score: the mean adjusted Rand index across these
full-cohort participant partitions. It can be negative when agreement is
worse than chance.
\code{stability_fractions} can request an explicit deletion curve; the existing
90\% fit-table fields remain the primary review metrics, while
\code{Stability$deletion_curve} records every requested retention level.
Co-membership, split/merge, and profile-recovery diagnostics describe
whether instability is localized despite imperfect exact membership; they
never replace all-participant ARI.
\code{StabilityJaccard_Mean} is the mean label-matched, per-profile Jaccard
recovery and is retained as a descriptive diagnostic. \code{ReproducibilityScore}
equals \code{StabilityARI_Mean}; \code{StabilitySuccessRate} is reported separately.
\code{Reproducibility_scaled} is
the candidate-table min-max rescaling used only by the AHP index and is not
an independently interpretable reproducibility measure.

The composite rank index (stored as \code{ahp_index}) is computed by:
\enumerate{
\item Z-scaling AIC, BIC, Entropy, and (when stability is run)
reproducibility across candidate solutions (AIC/BIC are negated so
that higher scaled scores are preferred).
\item Taking their weighted mean. Weights are equal unless
\code{criteria_weights} supplies them, either directly or as an
Analytic Hierarchy Process pairwise-comparison matrix.
\item Recommending the highest-scoring candidate among those marked
\code{Eligible} (see \code{min_cluster_prop}).
}
The chosen weights, their method, and any consistency ratio are returned in
\code{ModelInfo_MClust$AHP}.

\code{MinProfileParticipantN} and \code{MinProfileParticipantProportion}
give the size of the smallest profile in training participants rather than
SOM nodes.

Phenotypes are learned by Mclust on the SOM codebook. Hard assignment uses
the best-matching node's Mclust class. Soft assignment uses normalized
inverse-squared codebook-distance weights across the nearest nodes to
combine their Mclust posterior probabilities. \code{SoftEntropy} is the
normalized entropy of that participant-level probability vector
(entropy divided by \code{log(k)}) and \code{SoftMargin} is the largest minus
second-largest probability. \verb{prob_*} columns describe a SOM node's Mclust
posterior, whereas \verb{SoftProb_*} columns describe a participant's
proximity-weighted membership. Boundary flags are confidence diagnostics
only: they do not remove, relabel, or suppress a participant during
training, stability scoring, or projection.

Each new fit uses one deterministic, seeded full-cohort batch SOM. Its
initialization codebook, preprocessing, grid, topology, neighbourhood,
epochs, codebooks, and node-level mixture are retained so projection is
completely frozen. Earlier fitted objects remain projectable because
projection uses their frozen map and mixture rather than refitting either
component.

\code{SOM_Distance} is the Euclidean distance, in Z-score units, from a
participant to their best-matching unit. Its square is the sum of the
squared per-variable residuals, returned in \code{ModelInfo_SOM$SOMFit$residuals}
(\code{Resid_<variable>}). \code{ModelInfo_SOM$SOMFit$variable_contribution}
summarizes each variable's share of training misfit, and each participant's
largest contributor is recorded in \code{Top_Distance_Variable} and
\code{Top_Distance_Share}.

LPA model/profile combinations are fit one at a time so that failed or
warning-producing solutions are captured in diagnostics instead of blocking
the entire pipeline. Successful fits are retained and failed fits are listed
in \code{ModelInfo_MClust$diagnostics}.
}
\section{Model review and refinement}{

Start with \code{ModelInfo_MClust$fit_table}, the AHP recommendation, and
\code{fit_plot} to compare candidate model specifications and numbers of
profiles. AHP is a useful starting point, not an automatic final decision:
retain solutions whose fit, cluster size, and clinical interpretability are
all reasonable.

The Circular and Line SOM widgets show how the analysis variables vary across
the map; the Cloud widget shows the observations within map cells. Use them
to assess whether the candidate solution represents coherent, interpretable
phenotype patterns. Inspect \code{SOMFit$node_occupancy} for empty or sparse
cells, and the SOM-distance plots for clusters with systematically poor map
representation. The posterior-probability plots in \code{ProbFit$plots}
identify uncertain node- or individual-level assignments.

If these diagnostics suggest a weak solution, reconsider the variables, SOM
grid dimensions, model family, or candidate \code{k} range and refit in
exploratory mode. Once a solution is selected, refit it with
\code{method = "finalize"}, \code{final_k}, and \code{final_model} to create
the reusable model for projection.

Set \code{stability_resamples} to a positive value to assess internal
reproducibility. Each 90\% subsample refits the same candidate SOM/LPA solution
and projects the original participants back into it. Mean adjusted Rand
index summarizes whole-partition agreement; cluster-wise Jaccard recovery
identifies phenotypes that dissolve despite good overall agreement. This is
internal reproducibility, not independent-cohort validation.
}

\section{Reviewing the candidates}{

Exploratory mode fits every combination of \code{k} and model family, so the
comparison is made once, on one table, rather than by refitting by hand.
\code{ModelInfo_MClust$fit_table} holds every candidate solution with its
information criteria, entropy, subsample reproducibility, and the combined
AHP index side by side.

A candidate missing from that table is never a mystery:
\code{ModelInfo_MClust$diagnostics$lpa_fit_diagnostics} records each fit's
status, runtime, warnings, and errors, so model families that fail to
converge or exceed the fit timeout show up there.

Once a solution is chosen, refit it on its own with \code{method = "finalize"} to
get the reusable, projectable model.
}

\section{Stability output}{

Stability assesses internal reproducibility by full-pipeline 90\% participant
subsampling without replacement. For each replicate, 90\% of complete
participants are selected once, all preprocessing, any reduction (PCA,
MCA, or SOM), and the selected clustering method are refit. Most pipelines
compare assignments for sampled participants. SOM + Mclust instead maps the
complete reference cohort onto every refit and compares the resulting full
participant partition. \code{Stability$settings$comparison_scope} records the
policy used. It is an internal sensitivity analysis, not
independent-cohort validation.

\code{Stability$settings} records the analysis provenance:
\itemize{
\item \code{resamples}: requested number of 90\% subsample refits.
\item \code{seed}: seed used to derive reproducible sampling and model seeds.
\item \code{refit_scope}: whether agreement is calculated directly in the
refitted sample or after refit projection onto the reference cohort.
\item \code{comparison_scope}: the participants entering replicate agreement
metrics; most pipelines use \code{"sampled_participants"}, while SOM + Mclust
uses \code{"all_reference_participants"}.
\item \code{resample_type}: \code{"subsample_without_replacement"} for the
primary stability analysis.
\item \code{resample_fraction}: the retained participant fraction, \code{0.90}.
\item \code{resample_fractions}: every requested retention fraction. The
default is \code{0.90}; additional values create an opt-in deletion-stability
curve without replacing the 90\% primary screen.
\item \code{coassignment_limit}: maximum number of complete training
participants (2,000) for which the full pairwise co-assignment matrix is
calculated.
\item \code{noise_policy}: whether the method has noise labels. For ordinary
methods it is \code{"all clusters included"}; HDBSCAN variants retain noise in
ARI but exclude it from phenotype-level Jaccard recovery.
\item \code{requested_cores}, \code{resolved_cores}, and \code{worker_policy}: the
requested and resolved refit workers and the reason for the resolution.
Automatic resolution is capped by CPU, refit count, and the smaller of
50\% physical RAM and 75\% currently available RAM. Pairwise-memory methods
are run serially automatically.
\item \code{estimated_bytes_per_worker}, \code{estimated_total_bytes},
\code{physical_memory_bytes}, \code{available_memory_bytes}, and
\code{safe_memory_budget_bytes}: the conservative memory audit used to resolve
automatic workers. An explicit worker request is preserved (within the
CPU/scheduler cap) but warns if it exceeds the estimated safe budget.
}

\code{Stability$replicates} has one row per requested refit. \code{Model} and
\code{Classes} identify the selected candidate (for HDBSCAN, \code{Classes} is the
data-derived extracted count); \code{Replicate} is its sequence number; \code{Status}
is \code{"success"} or a failure status; \code{SamplingSeed} and \code{ModelSeed} identify
the participant draw and refit initialization policy; and \code{Error}
contains the error message for an unsuccessful refit. \code{ARI} is the primary
successful-refit metric: it is invariant to cluster numbering, higher is
better, and it can be negative when agreement is worse than chance.

\code{Stability$cluster_recovery} has one row for each reference \code{Cluster} in
each successful \code{Replicate}. \code{Jaccard} is the recovery of that reference
cluster after matching it to the subsample cluster with the largest Jaccard
overlap; higher is better and one is exact recovery. \code{Model} and \code{Classes}
again identify the fitted candidate.

\code{Stability$summary} combines successful subsample refits:
\code{StabilitySuccessRate} is successful refits divided by requested refits, an
operational reliability measure that does not enter the reproducibility
score. \code{StabilityARI_Mean} and \code{StabilityARI_P05} are the mean and fifth
percentile of ARI. \code{StabilityJaccard_Mean} and \code{StabilityJaccard_Min} are
respectively the mean cluster-level recovery and the minimum of the
cluster-specific mean recoveries across replicates.
\code{ReproducibilityScore} does not include success rate. For most pipelines it
is the mean of finite \code{StabilityARI_Mean} and \code{StabilityJaccard_Mean}
values; SOM + Mclust uses \code{StabilityARI_Mean} directly because it selects
on agreement of the final participant partition.

SOM + Mclust additionally returns \code{StabilityARI_NonBoundary},
\code{StabilityARI_NonBoundary_P05}, and non-boundary Jaccard summaries. These
are secondary diagnostics calculated only on the fixed reference-model mask
with \code{SoftMargin} below its threshold or \code{SoftEntropy} above its threshold
excluded. The primary all-participant ARI always retains these boundary
participants. \code{boundary_summary} reports their count and proportion overall
and within each reference cluster. Earlier posterior-based \code{Core} fields are
retained for compatibility as descriptive diagnostics; they are not the
boundary-aware sensitivity measure.

\code{Stability$failures} repeats the replicate columns for unsuccessful refits,
making fit failures auditable without mixing them with successful metrics.

\code{Stability$participant_inclusion} is one row per complete reference
participant. \code{.row_id} identifies the merge-safe input row, \code{Cluster} its
reference assignment, \code{SuccessfulRefits} the number of usable refits, and
\code{InclusionProbability} the proportion of those refits in which the
participant returned to that cluster's label-matched subsample cluster.
\code{Model} and \code{Classes} identify the candidate. Higher inclusion is better.

\code{Stability$cluster_inclusion} summarizes inclusion within each reference
\code{Cluster}: \code{MeanInclusion}, \code{P05Inclusion}, and \code{MinInclusion} are the mean,
fifth percentile, and minimum participant inclusion probabilities; \code{Model}
and \code{Classes} identify the candidate. Higher values indicate that all, not
only the average, of a cluster is recovered consistently.

\code{Stability$coassignment} is available only when the complete training cohort
has at most \code{coassignment_limit} participants. Each candidate entry has a
\code{status} of \code{"available"}, \code{"skipped"}, or \code{"not_available"}; \code{reason}
explains a non-available result; \code{matrix} is the pairwise probability that
two complete reference participants are assigned together across successful
subsample refits; and \code{.row_id} maps matrix rows and columns to the same
identifiers returned in \code{DataWithClusters} and \code{ProbFit$individual}. Higher
matrix values mean more consistent pairwise co-membership.
Where more than one candidate is summarized, entries are named by its
\code{Model_Classes} key. The matrix is diagnostic only and is never used for
selection.

SOM + Mclust additionally returns memory-safe final-partition diagnostics
for every cohort size. \code{co_membership_by_cluster} reports the proportion of
within-reference-cluster participant pairs retained together (\code{Cohesion}) and
the probability of co-assignment with outsiders (\code{CrossMixing}).
\code{co_membership_between_clusters} reports cross-reference-cluster
co-assignment probabilities. \code{split_merge_reference} and
\code{split_merge_refit} identify dominant sources, shares, and effective numbers
of groups, while \code{profile_recovery} reports matched-cluster correlation and
RMSE for mean clustering-variable profiles on the frozen reference scale.
These explain the nature of membership changes; they do not supersede ARI.

\code{deletion_curve} summarizes ARI, VI, NMI, and Fowlkes--Mallows agreement at
each requested participant-retention fraction. It is useful for describing
sensitivity to increasing cohort perturbation, not for selecting the least
demanding fraction.

\code{Stability$plots} contains \code{cluster_recovery} (per-cluster Jaccard),
\code{partition_metrics} (the ARI distribution), and
\code{cluster_inclusion}; it also contains a co-assignment heatmap when the
matrix is available. These diagnostics complement rather than replace ARI
and Jaccard: none can turn a poorly reproducible cluster into a stable
phenotype.

Metric sources: Hubert and Arabie (1985) define ARI; Jaccard (1901) defines
the overlap coefficient; and Monti et al. (2003) describe resampling-based
consensus co-assignment.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
data("SimulatedPhenotypeData")
df_Training <- dplyr::filter(SimulatedPhenotypeData, .data$Cohort == "Training")
vars_Numeric <- paste0("Var", 1:12)

# Example-only display helper (not exported by SciDataReportR): round the
# numeric columns and add a frozen header.
ShowFitTable <- function(x, height = "320px") {
  x <- dplyr::mutate(
    dplyr::as_tibble(x),
    dplyr::across(dplyr::where(is.numeric), \(v) round(v, 3))
  )
  htmltools::browsable(htmltools::HTML(as.character(
    FreezeTableHeader(x, height = height, full_width = TRUE)
  )))
}

# Exploratory mode: every combination of k and model family. Model 2 is
# omitted here because it is the slowest to fit and, on this data, reaches
# the fit timeout at every k without contributing a candidate.
review <- CreateClusterModel_SOM_MClust(
  data = df_Training,
  variables = vars_Numeric,
  method = "exploratory",
  k_range = 2:5,
  models = c(1, 3),
  som_xdim = 5,
  som_ydim = 5,
  stability_resamples = 2,
  stability_seed = 20260805,
  Relabel = FALSE,
  min_nodes_per_cluster = NULL
)

# Every candidate solution
ShowFitTable(review$ModelInfo_MClust$fit_table)

# Status, runtime, warnings, and errors for every attempted fit
ShowFitTable(
  review$ModelInfo_MClust$diagnostics$lpa_fit_diagnostics,
  height = "260px"
)

review$ModelInfo_MClust$AHP$recommendation
ShowFitTable(review$Stability$summary, height = "260px")
review$fit_plot
review$fit_plot

# Refit the chosen solution to get the projectable model
model <- CreateClusterModel_SOM_MClust(
  data = df_Training, variables = vars_Numeric, method = "finalize",
  final_k = 4, final_model = 1, som_xdim = 5, som_ydim = 5,
  stability_resamples = 2, Relabel = FALSE,
  min_nodes_per_cluster = NULL
)
ShowFitTable(model$ModelInfo_MClust$fit_table, height = "160px")
}
}
